# busybox init. Four kinds of entry, and the order among the KINDS matters
# more than the order within this file: init runs every sysinit entry to
# completion, in file order, before starting anything else; then every wait
# entry, each to completion; then fires every once entry without waiting for
# it; and only then starts (and, forever after, restarts on exit) every
# respawn entry. docs/BOOT.md has the full reasoning.
#
# sysinit: rcS. Mounts, the config partition, the management address, the
# switch SDK-init verbs -- everything the respawn entries below need already
# in place. Nothing starts until this returns, so anything that blocks here
# costs every way back into the device at once.
::sysinit:/etc/init.d/rcS

# once: rcS.pon, the PON steps (docs/BOOT.md, "The PON steps"). Runs
# concurrently with the respawn entries below coming up -- it does not wait
# for them, and only one step (gponact) waits, bounded, for omcid.
::once:/etc/init.d/rcS.pon

# once: slot-state.sh. Records which slot is running and whether sw_commit
# (in both environment copies) names it, into /var/run/odi-slot for the
# exporter and the web UI, and into /var/run/motd (/etc/motd) for ssh
# logins. Reads only; bounded; exits (docs/FLASHING.md, "Committing").
::once:/etc/scripts/slot-state.sh

# once: trial-guard.sh. On a trial boot only (not committed, the next boot
# goes to another slot), registers an odi_wdt client that resets the board
# 300 s later unless the management path is seen working both ways first,
# so a trial that boots but cannot be reached goes back to the committed
# slot by itself. Reads the state slot-state.sh writes; bounded; exits
# (docs/FLASHING.md, "The trial guard").
::once:/etc/scripts/trial-guard.sh

# No telnet: ssh (dropbear) is the only network login. The trial boot and
# its self-revert are the way back if ssh ever fails to come up.
# askfirst, not respawn: login gives up after 60 s at its prompt, so a
# respawned login on a console nobody uses exited and restarted every
# minute, two syslog lines each time, enough on their own to cycle the
# 64 KB ring in a few hours. askfirst waits for Enter, with no timeout, and
# only then runs login.
ttyS0::askfirst:/bin/login -p

# syslogd, klogd, ntpd, metricsd, confd, dropbear, omcid: each restarted the
# instant it exits, forever, by init itself -- no supervise.sh, no restart
# budget, no rate limit (docs/SETTINGS.md, "native over hand-rolled"). Every
# entry is a script, not the daemon directly: each checks its own
# /etc/config/<name>.off flag first (rootfs/skeleton/etc/scripts/svc-*.sh)
# and sets its oom_score_adj right before exec-ing the daemon in place
# (rootfs/skeleton/etc/scripts/respawn.sh), so the pid init tracks is always
# the daemon itself, never a parent that already exited.
#
# igmpd is opt-in (IGMP_SNOOPING=1, scripts/svc-igmpd.sh): off, the entry
# parks in respawn-off.sh and the switch floods multicast as before.
#
# syslogd/klogd/ntpd are new vs. the stock image, which has neither a
# syslog daemon nor an NTP client. ntpd's own script gates on NTP_SERVER being set in the config
# store (docs/SETTINGS.md); this entry is always here, static like every
# other inittab line, and does nothing until that key is set.
::respawn:/etc/scripts/svc-syslogd.sh
::respawn:/etc/scripts/svc-klogd.sh
::respawn:/etc/scripts/svc-ntpd.sh
::respawn:/etc/scripts/svc-metricsd.sh
::respawn:/etc/scripts/svc-confd.sh
::respawn:/etc/scripts/svc-dropbear.sh
::respawn:/etc/scripts/svc-omcid.sh
::respawn:/etc/scripts/svc-igmpd.sh

::restart:/sbin/init
::ctrlaltdel:/sbin/reboot
::shutdown:/bin/umount -a -r
